"""Grade 3: the core text of each lesson, written as a catechist would plan it."""
R = {
'3-1-1': dict(
 idea='God wants us to know him, so he speaks to us: in the beauty of creation, through the prophets, and most fully in his Son, Jesus. The Bible is God’s word written down by people he inspired, and the Church helps us understand it.',
 hook='Hold up a letter with a stamp and a sunset photo. Ask: “How do you find out what someone is like?” Friends tell us, show us, and are with us. God does all three: he made a world full of signs, he spoke through prophets, and he sent Jesus.',
 activity='“Ways God speaks” stations (20 min). Station 1: look at a leaf, a shell or a photo of stars and write “God shows us…”. Station 2: read Psalm 19:1–4 aloud in pairs. Station 3: read John 1:14 and draw Jesus as God’s Word. Rotate every 5 minutes. End by sharing one thing each station taught.',
 teach=['Revelation means God showing us who he is (CCC 50–53). He did it step by step: creation, Adam and Eve, Noah, Abraham, Moses, the prophets, and finally Jesus, his Word made flesh (CCC 65).',
        'Hebrews 1:1–2 gives the whole story in two verses: “In many and various ways God spoke… but in these last days he has spoken to us by a Son.” Read it slowly with the class.',
        'God is the author of the Bible and the human writers were real authors too (CCC 105–106). Say “inspired” means God breathed his message through them without taking away their personality.',
        'Watch for the idea that the Bible fell from the sky or that Catholics do not read it. The Church reads it at every Mass and invites everyone to read it daily.'],
 misc=['“God only spoke to people long ago.”', '“The Bible is just an old storybook.”'],
 mem='Hebrews 1:1–2 (first part): “In many and various ways God spoke… but in these last days he has spoken to us by a Son.”',
 assess='Ask each child to name two ways God speaks to us and give one example of each.'),
'3-1-2': dict(
 idea='The Bible is a library of 73 books, 46 in the Old Testament and 27 in the New. The Old Testament tells how God prepared the world for Jesus, and the New Testament tells about Jesus and the first Church. The Gospels are the heart of the Bible.',
 hook='Bring a Bible and a stack of 73 index cards, or a bookshelf. Ask: “Is this one book or many?” Explain that it is like a family library: history, songs, letters, stories, prophecies, all about God’s love.',
 activity='“Bible treasure hunt” (25 min). Give pairs a Bible and a list of 8 references (Genesis 1:1, Psalm 23:1, Isaiah 9:6, Matthew 5:3, Luke 2:11, John 3:16, Acts 2:1, Revelation 21:4). They find each, identify the section (Law, History, Wisdom, Prophets, Gospels, Acts, Letters, Revelation) and copy one phrase. Review how to read a reference: book, chapter, verse.',
 teach=['The Catholic Bible has 73 books: 46 in the Old Testament and 27 in the New (CCC 120). The books are grouped by kind: Law, history, wisdom and psalms, prophets, Gospels, Acts, letters, Revelation.',
        'The Church decided which books belong in the Bible, called the canon, guided by the Holy Spirit and by what the apostles handed on (CCC 120). The Old and New Testaments belong together (CCC 128–130).',
        'The Gospels have a special place because they tell us about Jesus’ life and words (CCC 125). We stand when the Gospel is read at Mass to honor Christ himself speaking.',
        'Teach reference reading as a skill. Many children have never opened a Bible. Let them practice and celebrate small wins.'],
 misc=['“The Old Testament doesn’t matter anymore.”', '“Catholic Bibles have the same number of books as all other Bibles.”'],
 mem='“Your word is a lamp to my feet and a light to my path.” (Psalm 119:105)',
 assess='Give each child a Bible and a reference to find. Watch their fingers: can they find book, chapter and verse?'),
'3-1-3': dict(
 idea='A covenant is a sacred promise that makes people into a family. God made covenants with Noah, Abraham, Moses and David, and Jesus makes the new and everlasting covenant by giving his body and blood.',
 hook='Shake hands, then show a wedding ring or a family photo. Ask: “What is a promise that makes people belong to each other?” Tell them God makes promises like that, and he never breaks them.',
 activity='“Covenant timeline” (25 min). On a long paper strip draw five markers: rainbow (Noah), stars (Abraham), tablets (Moses), crown (David), chalice (Jesus). For each, write God’s promise and the sign. Read Genesis 9:8–17 aloud and trace the rainbow. Close by reading the words of the Eucharistic cup from Luke 22:20.',
 teach=['A covenant is more than a contract: it makes people family (CCC 56–64). God promised Noah never to destroy the earth by flood, Abraham a great nation, Moses a law and a people, David a kingdom without end.',
        'Each covenant had a sign: the rainbow, circumcision, the Law written on tablets, the throne of David (CCC 56–62). In the new covenant the signs are the sacraments, above all the Eucharist (CCC 1365).',
        'Jesus said at the Last Supper, “This cup is the new covenant in my blood” (Luke 22:20). The Mass makes this covenant present each time we celebrate it (CCC 762).',
        'The idea that God changes his mind or abandons people is a common worry. Stress that God’s promises are faithful even when people fail.'],
 misc=['“God gets angry and breaks his promises when we sin.”', '“A covenant is just a rule.”'],
 mem='“This cup that is poured out for you is the new covenant in my blood.” (Luke 22:20)',
 assess='Ask children to match each person (Noah, Abraham, Moses, David) with his sign and a promise.'),
'3-2-1': dict(
 idea='The first three commandments teach us to love God above all: worship God alone, speak his name with reverence, and keep Sunday holy. They are not rules to hold us back but the way of a loving relationship.',
 hook='Ask who is the most important person in your life, then who comes second. Ask: “What would happen if something else took first place, like a video game?” Put a sign saying “GOD” on the table and ask children to add things they might put in first place.',
 activity='“First place” clay and prayer (25 min). Children make a small clay trophy stand with “1” on it. They list on a card things that can take God’s place (games, money, popularity) and fold it under the stand. Read Exodus 20:1–11. Practice saying “Jesus” and “God” with reverence, and plan one way to keep this Sunday holy.',
 teach=['The first commandment: “I am the Lord your God… you shall have no other gods before me” (CCC 2084–2094). We give God our faith, hope, love and worship, and we reject idols. An idol is anything we put before God.',
        'The second commandment: we honor God’s name (CCC 2142–2149). We do not curse or swear carelessly. We bless God’s name in the Sign of the Cross and the Our Father.',
        'The third commandment: keep holy the Lord’s Day (CCC 2168–2176). Sunday is the day of the Resurrection, so we go to Mass and rest from unnecessary work (CCC 2177–2185).',
        'Children often hear “commandments” as a list of no’s. Frame them as a love letter from a Father who knows what keeps us free and happy (CCC 2060–2063).'],
 misc=['“Using God’s name when I’m surprised is harmless.”', '“Sunday is just a day off.”'],
 mem='“You shall love the Lord your God with all your heart.” (Deuteronomy 6:5)',
 assess='Each child says one thing they will put first, one way to respect God’s name, and one way to keep Sunday special.'),
'3-2-2': dict(
 idea='The other seven commandments show how to love our neighbor: honor parents, respect life, be faithful and pure, be honest and fair, and be generous and content. Jesus summed them up in one word: love.',
 hook='Act out two scenes: a child shares a snack and another grabs one. Ask which is love. Then ask: “If we had to make rules for love, what would they be?” Show that the commandments are God’s answer.',
 activity='“Commandment charades” (25 min). Write commandments 4 to 10 on cards in kid language (obey and honor; do not hurt; be faithful and kind with your body; do not steal; tell the truth; do not be jealous; be happy with what you have). In teams, act out the right choice for a school situation while others guess the commandment. Finish with each child writing one “love action” for the week.',
 teach=['The fourth commandment: honor your father and mother (CCC 2196–2257 treats the whole law of love of neighbor; 2214–2220 treats honoring parents). Honor includes obedience, gratitude and care.',
        'The fifth: do not kill (CCC 2258–2330). Every human life is sacred from beginning to natural end, so we do not hurt others, and we care for our own health.',
        'The sixth to tenth: be faithful and pure (CCC 2331–2400), do not steal (CCC 2401–2463), do not lie (CCC 2464–2513), and do not envy (CCC 2514–2557). Use child-friendly examples.',
        'Jesus gave a summary: love God and love your neighbor as yourself (Matthew 22:37–40; CCC 2055). Every commandment is a way to love.'],
 misc=['“I only need to follow the commandments that are easy.”', '“Little lies don’t matter.”'],
 mem='“Love your neighbor as yourself.” (Mark 12:31)',
 assess='Give each child a situation (a friend is left out; you found money) and ask which commandment helps and what they would do.'),
'3-2-3': dict(
 idea='Conscience is the voice inside us that helps us know right from wrong. It must be trained, like a muscle, by prayer, the Bible, the teaching of the Church and the advice of good people. We always follow our conscience, and we always work to form it well.',
 hook='Show two compasses: one that works and one that is bent. Ask which you would trust to find the way home. Explain that conscience is like a compass; it must be set by God’s truth.',
 activity='“Compass check” (20 min). Present three situations (a cashier gives extra change; a friend asks you to cheat; a classmate is teased). For each, children stand at one of four corners: Pray, Read what the Church teaches, Ask a wise person, Think about the effects. Discuss why each tool helps. Then each child writes “One person I can ask when I am unsure is…”.',
 teach=['Conscience is a judgment of reason that tells us the moral quality of an act (CCC 1776–1778). “Deep within his conscience man discovers a law which he has not laid upon himself” (CCC 1776).',
        'We must always obey our conscience, and we must form it (CCC 1783–1785). We form it by listening to God’s word, praying, asking for advice, and examining our actions each day.',
        'Romans 2:15 says the law is written on our hearts. Even people who have not heard the Gospel have a conscience. The Holy Spirit helps us hear it clearly.',
        'Do not confuse conscience with feelings. A person can feel fine about a wrong choice. That is why we learn what is true.'],
 misc=['“My conscience is just what I feel like.”', '“If nobody sees me, it doesn’t matter.”'],
 mem='Come, Holy Spirit, fill the hearts of your faithful, and kindle in them the fire of your love.',
 assess='Present a new situation and ask which tools of conscience they would use, and why.'),
'3-3-1': dict(
 idea='The Beatitudes are Jesus’ eight surprising promises of happiness. They describe the life of a disciple and point toward heaven: blessed are the poor in spirit, those who mourn, the meek, the hungry for justice, the merciful, the pure in heart, the peacemakers, and the persecuted.',
 hook='Ask: “What do people say makes you happy?” (stuff, winning, being popular). Then read Matthew 5:3 aloud: “Blessed are the poor in spirit.” Ask: “Does that sound like what the world says?” Let them wonder.',
 activity='“Beatitude picture book” (30 min). In pairs, each takes one Beatitude, restates it in child words, draws a scene showing someone living it, and names a real person (a saint, a family member) who is like that. Bind the pages as a class book and read it aloud at the end.',
 teach=['The Beatitudes (Matthew 5:3–12) are the heart of Jesus’ preaching (CCC 1716). They describe Christ’s own life and the path to heaven, and answer our desire for happiness.',
        '“Blessed” means truly happy. The Beatitudes show that real happiness comes from God and from loving others, not from having more (CCC 1718–1719, 1725).',
        'Let children match a saint to each: Francis of Assisi (poor in spirit), Teresa of Calcutta (merciful), Maria Goretti (pure in heart), John XXIII (peacemaker), Thomas More (persecuted for righteousness).',
        'The last Beatitude, persecution, may frighten children. Say that being a friend of Jesus is sometimes hard, and that he promises to be with us.'],
 misc=['“Being meek means being weak.”', '“Only grown-ups can live the Beatitudes.”'],
 mem='“Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God.” (Matthew 5:8)',
 assess='Each child chooses one Beatitude, says it in their own words and names one action to live it this week.'),
'3-3-2': dict(
 idea='Faith, hope and love are the theological virtues. God gives them to us at Baptism so we can believe in him, trust his promises and love him and others as he loves us. The greatest is love.',
 hook='Show three gifts in a box labeled Faith, Hope, Love. Ask what each might hold. Open them: a pair of glasses (faith sees what we cannot), an anchor (hope holds), a heart (love gives).',
 activity='“Virtue chain” (25 min). Provide paper links in three colors. For each virtue, children write an action that shows it at home, at school, at church. Link the chain around the room. Discuss: how do we grow in a virtue? (practice, prayer, sacraments).',
 teach=['Theological virtues come directly from God and lead to God (CCC 1812–1813). They are given at Baptism and grow through prayer and the sacraments.',
        'Faith is believing God and all he has revealed (CCC 1814). Hope is trusting his promises and desiring heaven (CCC 1817). Charity, or love, is loving God above all and neighbor as ourselves (CCC 1822).',
        '1 Corinthians 13:13: “faith, hope and love abide, these three; but the greatest of these is love.” Love is greatest because it lasts forever (CCC 1826).',
        'Virtues are habits: we get better with practice. Connect to sports or music so children see the pattern.'],
 misc=['“Hope just means wishing.”', '“Love is a feeling.”'],
 mem='“Faith, hope and love abide, these three; but the greatest of these is love.” (1 Corinthians 13:13)',
 assess='Ask each child to give a situation where they could show faith, one for hope and one for love.'),
'3-3-3': dict(
 idea='The four cardinal virtues are prudence (choosing wisely), justice (giving others what they deserve), fortitude (being brave) and temperance (self-control). They are the “hinges” of a good life, and with God’s help they become habits.',
 hook='Show a door and its hinge. Explain that “cardinal” comes from the Latin for hinge: these four virtues are like hinges on which a good life swings. Open and close the door several times.',
 activity='“Four hinges” relay (25 min). Four stations, one per virtue, each with a scenario card (e.g., “You see a younger child being teased”). At each, a team decides what the virtue looks like and acts it out. Then everyone writes a personal goal: “I will practice [virtue] by…”.',
 teach=['Prudence helps us see what is good and choose the right way (CCC 1806). Justice gives God and neighbor what is due (CCC 1807). Fortitude gives courage in difficulty (CCC 1808). Temperance moderates our desires (CCC 1809).',
        'Wisdom 8:7 says that wisdom teaches “self-control and prudence, justice and courage.” These virtues were known to the Greeks and then deepened by Christ (CCC 1805).',
        'Virtues are built by repeated good acts and by grace. Saints like Thomas More (justice), Joan of Arc (fortitude) and Francis de Sales (gentleness) can be stories.',
        'Children may think virtues are about being perfect. Stress that we grow bit by bit, and that mistakes are chances to try again.'],
 misc=['“Being brave means never being afraid.”', '“Self-control is no fun.”'],
 mem='“Be strong and of good courage… for the Lord your God is with you wherever you go.” (Joshua 1:9)',
 assess='Give scenarios and ask which virtue each calls for.'),
'3-4-1': dict(
 idea='Jesus is true God and true man. The eternal Son of God became a human being, was born of Mary, and lived among us. He did this to save us and to show us the Father.',
 hook='Hold two objects: a toy knight’s crown and a plain cloak. Say: “Imagine a king who puts on a servant’s clothes to live among his people.” Then read John 1:14. Jesus did something even greater.',
 activity='“Two columns” poster (25 min). Children make a poster with two columns: “Jesus is human” (hungry, tired, cried, had a mother) and “Jesus is God” (forgave sins, calmed storms, rose from the dead). Each child adds evidence from a Gospel story assigned to them: Mark 4:35–41, John 11:35, Luke 7:48.',
 teach=['The Incarnation: God’s Son became man (CCC 461–463). He remained God and took a human nature, so he is one Person with two natures (CCC 464–469).',
        'Jesus experienced hunger, tiredness, joy and sorrow (CCC 470–478). He also forgave sins and commanded storms, showing he is God.',
        'Why did he become man? To save us, to show God’s love, to be our model of holiness, and to make us sharers in God’s life (CCC 457–460).',
        'Avoid either error: that Jesus only seemed human, or that he was just a good man. Use plain words.'],
 misc=['“Jesus was only pretending to be human.”', '“Jesus was a good teacher, but not God.”'],
 mem='“And the Word became flesh and dwelt among us.” (John 1:14)',
 assess='Ask: “Name one thing that shows Jesus is human and one that shows he is God.”'),
'3-4-2': dict(
 idea='Jesus announced that God’s kingdom had come, and he showed it by teaching and by miracles. His miracles are signs that point to who he is and call us to trust him.',
 hook='Read the first lines of Mark 4:35–41, then stop at the storm. Ask what they would do. Then read the end: “Who then is this, that even the wind and the sea obey him?”',
 activity='“Sign or magic?” sort (20 min). Provide cards of Gospel miracles (calming the storm, feeding five thousand, healing the blind man, raising Jairus’ daughter). For each, children write what it shows about Jesus (power over nature, compassion, power over death) and what it asks of us (trust). Then compare with magic tricks: what is different?',
 teach=['Jesus preached the Kingdom of God with parables and with actions (CCC 541–546). Miracles were “works of God” that showed the Kingdom had come (CCC 547–550).',
        'Miracles are signs. They invite faith; they do not force it (CCC 548). Jesus did not use miracles to show off but to heal and free (CCC 549).',
        'Jesus’ biggest sign is his Resurrection. Healing and forgiveness connect: he healed bodies as a sign of healing souls (Mark 2:1–12).',
        'Children may ask why Jesus doesn’t heal everyone today. Answer honestly: God cares, he works in many ways, and he asks us to be his hands.'],
 misc=['“Miracles were magic tricks.”', '“If I pray hard enough, God must do what I ask.”'],
 mem='“Who then is this, that even the wind and the sea obey him?” (Mark 4:41)',
 assess='Ask children to name one miracle, what it shows about Jesus, and how we should respond.'),
'3-4-3': dict(
 idea='Jesus died on the cross to forgive our sins and rose from the dead on the third day. His Resurrection shows he is God, defeats death, and opens heaven to us. Easter is the greatest feast.',
 hook='Show a seed in dry dirt. Say: “It looks dead, but watch.” Show a picture of a plant. Read John 12:24 (“Unless a grain of wheat falls into the earth and dies…”). Say, “Jesus is the seed that gives life.”',
 activity='“Three days” storyboard (25 min). Fold a paper into three: Friday (the Cross), Saturday (the tomb), Sunday (the empty tomb). Children draw each scene and write one sentence. Read Luke 23:33–46 and 24:1–12 in pieces. End with a joyful “Alleluia!” as they unveil the Sunday panel.',
 teach=['Jesus died “for our sins” (1 Corinthians 15:3; CCC 599–605). His death was a free gift of love (CCC 609–614).',
        'On the third day he rose in his glorified body (CCC 638–644). He ate with his disciples, and they touched him. The empty tomb and his appearances are the foundation of our faith.',
        'The Resurrection means death is not the end, sin is forgiven and heaven is open (CCC 651–655). Every Sunday is a “little Easter.”',
        'Be gentle with Good Friday. Children can be upset by the Cross. Emphasize love and the victory of Easter.'],
 misc=['“Jesus only pretended to die.”', '“Easter is mostly about bunnies and eggs.”'],
 mem='“He is not here; he has risen!” (Luke 24:6)',
 assess='Have children retell the story in order and tell why Easter is joyful.'),
'3-5-1': dict(
 idea='On Pentecost the Holy Spirit came upon Mary and the apostles like wind and fire. They were filled with courage, preached to the crowds, and the Church began. The same Spirit is given to us in Baptism and Confirmation.',
 hook='Open a window or use a small fan and a candle (safely). Say: “You can’t see wind but you can see what it does.” Light the candle. Say the Spirit is like both: strong and warm. Then read Acts 2:1–4.',
 activity='“Pentecost on the move” (25 min). Cut red and orange paper flames. On each flame, children write a gift they need from the Spirit (courage, wisdom, kindness). Tell Acts 2 with sounds: wind (whooshing), fire (clap), languages (greeting in different languages). Hang the flames over a picture of the Church.',
 teach=['The Holy Spirit is the third Person of the Trinity, God like the Father and Son (CCC 685–687). At Pentecost he filled the apostles and made the Church visible to the world (CCC 731–732).',
        'Symbols of the Spirit: wind, fire, water, oil, dove (CCC 694–701). Teach two or three that children can see in church.',
        'Before Pentecost the apostles hid; afterward they spoke boldly. The Spirit gives courage, truth and unity.',
        'Avoid presenting the Spirit as a force. He is a Person who loves, teaches and guides.'],
 misc=['“The Holy Spirit is just a feeling.”', '“Pentecost was only for the apostles.”'],
 mem='Come, Holy Spirit, fill the hearts of your faithful, and kindle in them the fire of your love.',
 assess='Ask: “What happened at Pentecost, and how does the Spirit help us today?”'),
'3-5-2': dict(
 idea='The Church Jesus founded is one, holy, catholic and apostolic. One because she has one Lord, one faith, one Baptism; holy because Christ makes her holy; catholic because she is for everyone everywhere; apostolic because she is built on the apostles and their successors.',
 hook='Show four photos: one family at a table (one), a stained-glass saint (holy), a world map (catholic), a line of people passing a torch (apostolic). Ask what word each might show.',
 activity='“Four marks puzzle” (25 min). Cut a church picture into four pieces labeled One, Holy, Catholic, Apostolic. Children complete it by reading a clue at each piece (Ephesians 4:4–6 for One; Matthew 28:19 for Catholic; Ephesians 2:20 for Apostolic) and giving an example.',
 teach=['The Creed names four marks of the Church (CCC 811–870). They help us recognize the Church Jesus founded.',
        'One: one Lord, one faith, one Baptism (CCC 813–815). Holy: God is holy and he makes his people holy; saints show it (CCC 823–829). Catholic means “universal”: for all people and with the fullness of the faith (CCC 830–831).',
        'Apostolic: built on the apostles, with bishops as their successors and the Pope as successor of Peter (CCC 857–865).',
        'Avoid suggesting other Christians are not Christians. Say we are united by Baptism, and pray for unity.'],
 misc=['“Catholic means ‘Roman’ only.”', '“Holy means everyone in the Church is perfect.”'],
 mem='“Lord, I believe in one, holy, catholic and apostolic Church.” (from the Nicene Creed)',
 assess='Give each mark and ask for its meaning and an example.'),
'3-5-3': dict(
 idea='The Church is a family with different members. The Pope leads the whole Church as successor of Peter, bishops lead dioceses as successors of the apostles, priests and deacons serve, and all the baptized (the laity) share Jesus’ mission in the world.',
 hook='Show a body outline and ask: “What if the foot said it wasn’t needed?” Read 1 Corinthians 12:12–14. Ask what each part of the Church does.',
 activity='“Church family tree” (25 min). Children build a chart: Jesus, Peter and the apostles, Pope, bishop of your diocese (find the name), your pastor, deacons, parents and teachers, children. Under each, write what they do (teach, lead, celebrate sacraments, serve). Add “my part is…”.',
 teach=['Christ is the head and the Church is his Body (CCC 791–795). The Pope, bishop of Rome, is the successor of St Peter and has care for the whole Church (CCC 880–882).',
        'Bishops teach, sanctify and govern; priests help the bishop and celebrate Mass and sacraments; deacons serve at the altar, the Word and charity (CCC 874–896).',
        'The laity make up most of the Church and share in Christ’s mission to be priest, prophet and king in daily life (CCC 897–913).',
        'Name your bishop and pastor. Children love knowing real names.'],
 misc=['“Only priests and nuns have jobs in the Church.”', '“The Pope is the Church.”'],
 mem='“We are many parts, but one body in Christ.” (see Romans 12:5)',
 assess='Ask children to name three roles in the Church and what each does.'),
'3-6-1': dict(
 idea='Mary is the Mother of God because her Son Jesus is God. She believed God’s word, said yes at the Annunciation, and stood by Jesus to the cross. She is also our mother, and we honor her and ask her to pray for us.',
 hook='Ask children who taught them to say “yes” when it was hard. Show a picture of the Annunciation and ask what Mary says. Read Luke 1:38.',
 activity='“Mary’s yes” frieze (25 min). Divide Mary’s life into six panels: Annunciation, Visitation, Nativity, Finding in the Temple, Cana, the Cross. Each group paints a panel and writes one thing Mary teaches. Pray the Hail Mary together slowly, noting its two halves (the angel’s words and our petition).',
 teach=['Mary is “Theotokos,” Mother of God, because Jesus is God (CCC 495). The Church honors her above all other saints but never worships her (CCC 971).',
        'Mary is a model of faith. At Cana she says, “Do whatever he tells you” (John 2:5). At the Cross she shares Jesus’ suffering. At Pentecost she prays with the apostles.',
        'At the Cross Jesus gave her to John, and to all of us, as mother (John 19:26–27; CCC 501, 963). We ask her to pray for us as we ask any friend.',
        'Some children’s families are not Catholic. Explain that honoring Mary honors Jesus.'],
 misc=['“Catholics worship Mary.”', '“Mary is more important than Jesus.”'],
 mem='Hail Mary, full of grace, the Lord is with thee; blessed art thou among women, and blessed is the fruit of thy womb, Jesus.',
 assess='Ask: “Why is Mary called Mother of God? What does she teach us?”'),
'3-6-2': dict(
 idea='The communion of saints is the whole family of God: the faithful on earth, those being purified in purgatory, and the saints in heaven. We pray for each other, and the saints pray for us.',
 hook='Show a photo of family members, some far away or who have died. Ask, “Are they still part of your family?” Introduce the idea that God’s family stretches beyond what we see.',
 activity='“Three circles” (25 min). Draw three concentric circles: earth, purgatory, heaven. Children write names in each: people they pray for, people who have died they pray for, saints they ask to pray for them. Read Hebrews 12:1 (“a great cloud of witnesses”). Pray the Litany of the Saints in a short form.',
 teach=['The Church is one family in Christ, in three states: pilgrims on earth, souls being purified, and saints in glory (CCC 954).',
        'Saints are people who lived heroic love and now see God (CCC 828, 956). They pray for us, and we can ask for their help (CCC 956–957).',
        'We pray for the dead, especially at Mass and on All Souls (November 2). Purgatory is the final cleansing of those who die in God’s friendship (CCC 1030–1031).',
        'Be gentle with children who have lost someone. Say that love does not end.'],
 misc=['“Praying to saints takes away from Jesus.”', '“Only people in heaven are in the Church.”'],
 mem='“Therefore, since we are surrounded by so great a cloud of witnesses…” (Hebrews 12:1)',
 assess='Ask children to name the three parts of the communion of saints and one person to pray for.'),
'3-6-3': dict(
 idea='God made us to be with him forever. Heaven is perfect life with God, and Jesus opened it to us. Our choices matter: we can choose God or turn away. God offers mercy, and we hope to be with him forever.',
 hook='Ask: “What is the best party you can imagine?” Then read Revelation 21:4 aloud. Explain that heaven is even better because God is there.',
 activity='“Road to heaven” map (25 min). Children draw a winding road with signs for choices (honest word, helping a friend, sharing, praying) that lead toward a bright city and signs (lying, hurting) that lead away, and a path back (sorry, Confession). Read Revelation 21:1–4. Talk about how Jesus helps us stay on the road.',
 teach=['Heaven is the fulfillment of our deepest longing: to see God and live with him (CCC 1023–1026). It is a life of love and joy that has no end.',
        'God desires all to be saved (CCC 1058). We can freely refuse him. Hell is the choice to stay away from God forever (CCC 1033–1037). Speak gently and briefly with children.',
        'After death each person is judged by Christ (CCC 1021–1022). We trust in his mercy and live for him now.',
        'Do not use fear. Teach that the aim of the Christian life is love of God and joy.'],
 misc=['“Heaven is floating on clouds.”', '“Good people automatically go to heaven.”'],
 mem='“God will wipe away every tear from their eyes.” (Revelation 21:4)',
 assess='Ask children to say what heaven is and one choice that leads toward God.'),
'3-7-1': dict(
 idea='A sacrament is an outward sign, given by Christ, that gives us God’s grace. The Church has seven: Baptism, Confirmation, Eucharist, Reconciliation, Anointing of the Sick, Holy Orders and Matrimony.',
 hook='Show a handshake, a hug and a ring. Ask what each means. Explain that signs do something real. Sacraments are signs that actually give what they show.',
 activity='“Seven signs” gallery (30 min). Seven stations with a real object each (water, chrism oil or scented oil, bread, a stole, oil of the sick, a ring, a candle). Children match each to its sacrament and write the sign and the grace. Make an accordion book of the seven.',
 teach=['A sacrament is “an efficacious sign of grace, instituted by Christ and entrusted to the Church” (CCC 1131). It uses material things (water, oil, bread) and words.',
        'The seven are grouped: sacraments of initiation (Baptism, Confirmation, Eucharist), of healing (Reconciliation, Anointing), of service (Holy Orders, Matrimony) (CCC 1210–1211).',
        'The sacraments are ways Christ touches us today, as he touched the sick in the Gospels (Mark 6:56; CCC 1115–1116).',
        'Do not reduce sacraments to ceremonies. They are encounters with Jesus.'],
 misc=['“Sacraments are just symbols.”', '“You earn grace by doing sacraments right.”'],
 mem='The seven sacraments: Baptism, Confirmation, Eucharist, Reconciliation, Anointing of the Sick, Holy Orders, Matrimony.',
 assess='Ask children to name all seven and match two with their signs.'),
'3-7-2': dict(
 idea='Baptism, Confirmation and the Eucharist are the sacraments of initiation. They bring us into the Church, make us God’s children, strengthen us with the Spirit and feed us with Jesus’ Body and Blood.',
 hook='Show a school ID, a team jersey and a key. Ask: “How do you join something?” Explain that these three sacraments “join” us to Jesus and his family.',
 activity='“Door, strength, meal” (25 min). Draw a church door (Baptism: new life in water), a flame (Confirmation: Spirit’s seal), a table (Eucharist: food for the journey). Under each, write the sign (water, oil, bread and wine), the words and the gift. Read Acts 2:38–42.',
 teach=['Baptism frees us from original sin, makes us children of God and members of the Church (CCC 1213, 1250). The sign is water and the words “I baptize you in the name of the Father…”',
        'Confirmation seals us with the gift of the Holy Spirit through anointing with chrism (CCC 1285, 1302–1303). The Eucharist is the source and summit of Christian life (CCC 1324).',
        'These three are received in a different order depending on your Church: in the Latin Church, Baptism, then First Communion and Confirmation (or Confirmation first in some places). Check your diocese.',
        'Invite families to share their baptism stories and photos.'],
 misc=['“You can ‘earn’ Baptism by being good.”', '“Confirmation is graduation from religious ed.”'],
 mem='“Go therefore and make disciples of all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit.” (Matthew 28:19)',
 assess='Ask children to name the three and the sign of each.'),
'3-7-3': dict(
 idea='Reconciliation and the Anointing of the Sick heal us in body and soul, and Holy Orders and Matrimony are for service: priests serve God’s people, and husband and wife serve each other and their children.',
 hook='Show a first-aid kit and a toolbox. “One mends, one builds.” The sacraments of healing mend us; the sacraments of service build up the Church.',
 activity='“Healing and service” sort (25 min). Cards with scenes (a sick grandfather, a child who lied, a wedding, an ordination). Children sort into Healing or Service and name the sacrament, minister and sign. Role-play a simple Confession examination (“Did I hurt someone? Did I tell the truth?”).',
 teach=['In Reconciliation, the priest acts in Christ’s name to forgive sins (CCC 1421–1424). The sign is the penitent’s confession, the priest’s absolution and the penance.',
        'In the Anointing of the Sick, the priest anoints the forehead and hands with oil and prays for strength and healing (CCC 1499–1513, James 5:14–15).',
        'Holy Orders gives bishops, priests and deacons their ministry (CCC 1536). Matrimony makes a man and a woman a lifelong covenant of love open to children (CCC 1601–1603).',
        'Introduce Confession as a gift. Reassure children that the priest is bound to secrecy.'],
 misc=['“Confession is only for big sins.”', '“The Anointing of the Sick is only for people who are dying.”'],
 mem='“Lord, I am sorry for my sins. Help me to do better.” (a child’s Act of Contrition, simple form)',
 assess='Ask each child to match a need with the sacrament that helps.'),
'3-8-1': dict(
 idea='Prayer is raising our hearts and minds to God. God invites us to pray, and he always hears us. We can pray anywhere, anytime, with words or in silence.',
 hook='Show a phone. Ask who calls them when they are away. “Prayer is a call to someone who loves us and always answers.” Pause for a moment of silence together.',
 activity='“Prayer rhythms” (20 min). Children draw a day: morning, meals, school, bedtime. For each, they write a short prayer (Morning Offering, Grace, Guardian Angel, Examen). They practice a minute of silence and share how it felt. Read Luke 11:1–4.',
 teach=['Prayer is “the raising of one’s mind and heart to God” (CCC 2559). It is a gift: God thirsts that we may thirst for him (CCC 2560).',
        'Jesus taught the Our Father when his friends asked, “Lord, teach us to pray” (Luke 11:1). We learn to pray by praying.',
        'Prayer has many forms but one meaning: a relationship. Good habits (a regular time, a quiet place) help (CCC 2691).',
        'Many children think prayer is only asking for things. Introduce listening and thanking.'],
 misc=['“God only answers if I pray the right words.”', '“Prayer is only for church.”'],
 mem='“Lord, teach us to pray.” (Luke 11:1)',
 assess='Ask each child to name when they will pray and what they will say.'),
'3-8-2': dict(
 idea='The Church names five kinds of prayer: blessing and adoration, petition, intercession, thanksgiving and praise. We can pray aloud (vocal prayer), by thinking about God’s word (meditation), or by resting in his love (contemplation).',
 hook='Play five short clips (or act): saying “wow” at a sunset, asking a parent for help, asking help for a friend, saying thank you, shouting for joy at a game. “Which is prayer?” Each can be.',
 activity='“Five forms” prayer corners (25 min). Five corners, each with a card and a way to pray: bless/adore (kneel and be quiet), petition (write one need), intercession (write a name), thanks (list three thanks), praise (sing or read Psalm 150). Rotate; then compose a class prayer using all five.',
 teach=['Five forms of prayer (CCC 2626–2643): adoration, petition, intercession, thanksgiving, praise. Each has a place in the Mass.',
        'Three expressions of prayer (CCC 2700–2724): vocal, meditation, contemplation. Meditation uses thought and imagination; contemplation is simple presence.',
        'Psalm 150 is a prayer of praise. The psalms are the prayer book of Jesus and the Church (CCC 2585–2589).',
        'Remind children that sorry (contrition) belongs to prayer too. It appears at the Mass and in Confession.'],
 misc=['“If God doesn’t give me what I ask, prayer doesn’t work.”', '“Prayer must be long.”'],
 mem='“Praise the Lord! Praise God in his sanctuary.” (Psalm 150:1)',
 assess='Ask children to match five prayer lines with their kinds.'),
'3-8-3': dict(
 idea='The Rosary is a prayer with Mary that helps us think about the life of Jesus. We pray Our Fathers, Hail Marys and Glory Bes while meditating on 20 mysteries in four sets: joyful, luminous, sorrowful, glorious.',
 hook='Hold up a rosary and a string of beads from a craft kit. Ask what the beads are for. Show how they help us count prayers while our minds rest in Jesus.',
 activity='“Make and pray” (30 min). Children make a decade rosary with ten beads, knot and cross. Teach how to hold it and say a decade, meditating on one mystery. Provide picture cards of the joyful mysteries. Pray one decade together slowly. Invite them to pray a decade at home with family.',
 teach=['The Rosary is a Gospel prayer that follows Mary as she contemplates Christ (CCC 971, 2678, 2708). It has 20 mysteries in four sets (St John Paul II added the luminous mysteries in 2002).',
        'The Hail Mary has two parts: the greeting of Gabriel and Elizabeth (Luke 1:28, 42) and the Church’s petition (CCC 2676–2677).',
        'We do not repeat prayers mindlessly: like a song, the words support the heart. Encourage children to picture each scene.',
        'Be kind to children who find sitting still hard; let them hold beads or follow with pictures.'],
 misc=['“The Rosary is just repeating words.”', '“You have to pray the whole Rosary every time.”'],
 mem='Glory be to the Father, and to the Son, and to the Holy Spirit, as it was in the beginning, is now, and ever shall be, world without end. Amen.',
 assess='Ask children to name the four sets of mysteries and one mystery from each.'),
'3-9-1': dict(
 idea='Sunday is the Lord’s Day, the day of Jesus’ Resurrection. Catholics gather at Mass on Sunday, hear God’s word, receive Jesus in the Eucharist, and rest and enjoy family. Missing Mass without a serious reason is a serious matter.',
 hook='Ask what makes a day special (birthday, holiday). Ask what makes Sunday different. Show a calendar with Sundays circled in gold: “Every Sunday is a little Easter.”',
 activity='“Make Sunday special” plan (25 min). Children design a Sunday poster with four boxes: Mass, rest, family, service. Brainstorm what each looks like (read a book, call a grandparent, visit someone). Write a family agreement they can bring home.',
 teach=['The Lord’s Day is Sunday because Jesus rose on the first day of the week (CCC 2174). Christians gather for the Eucharist, “the source and summit” of Church life (CCC 2177–2178).',
        'The Church obliges Catholics to attend Mass on Sundays and holy days unless excused for serious reasons such as illness (CCC 2180–2183).',
        'Rest is also holy: we rest from unnecessary work so we can worship, spend time with family and enjoy God’s gifts (CCC 2184–2188).',
        'Be sensitive: some families work on Sundays or lack transportation. Focus on desire, not blame.'],
 misc=['“Sunday Mass is just a rule.”', '“Rest means doing nothing.”'],
 mem='“This is the day that the Lord has made; let us rejoice and be glad in it.” (Psalm 118:24)',
 assess='Ask children to say why Sunday is special and what they will do this Sunday.'),
'3-9-2': dict(
 idea='The Church year retells the story of Jesus in seasons: Advent (purple), Christmas (white), Ordinary Time (green), Lent (purple), the Easter Triduum and Easter (white), and Pentecost (red). The colors of the priest’s vestments show the season.',
 hook='Show a color wheel and colored fabric swatches. Say: “The Church tells the story of Jesus all year long, with colors.” Ask which color might mean joy, waiting, fire.',
 activity='“Church year wheel” (30 min). Divide a paper plate or circle into seasons. Children color each section the right color, name the season, and write or draw one thing we do (wreath, manger, ashes, Easter candle). Display and use all year.',
 teach=['The liturgical year unfolds the whole mystery of Christ from the Incarnation to the Lord’s return (CCC 1163–1167). Sunday is the heart of it, and Easter is the greatest feast (CCC 1168–1169).',
        'Colors: purple (waiting and repentance), white (joy and purity), green (growth), red (Spirit and martyrs) (CCC 1155). Pink on two Sundays shows joy.',
        'The year begins with Advent, not January 1. Three days before Easter, the Triduum, is the center.',
        'Use your parish’s actual environment, e.g., take children to see the vestments or the paschal candle.'],
 misc=['“The Church year is just decoration.”', '“Lent is only about giving up candy.”'],
 mem='“For everything there is a season, and a time for every matter under heaven.” (Ecclesiastes 3:1)',
 assess='Ask children to match seasons, colors and one tradition each.'),
'3-9-3': dict(
 idea='Besides Sundays, the Church keeps feasts of Mary and the saints, and holy days of obligation when Catholics go to Mass. The saints’ feasts remind us that ordinary people can become holy with God’s help.',
 hook='Bring a calendar and open to your birthday. Ask which saint shares your day or name. Show how the Church has a feast almost every day.',
 activity='“My saint” cards (25 min). Each child picks a saint (by name day, patron of something, or heroes they like). They make a card with the saint’s picture, dates, how they loved God and a prayer. Then plan a “feast day” celebration for one of them.',
 teach=['Holy days of obligation in the U.S.: Mary Mother of God (Jan 1), Ascension, Assumption (Aug 15), All Saints (Nov 1), Immaculate Conception (Dec 8), Christmas (CCC 1167, 2043). Check your diocese for changes.',
        'On the feasts of saints the Church celebrates their “birthday into heaven” (CCC 1173). Their lives show different ways of following Christ.',
        'Revelation 7:9 shows the “great multitude” of saints. The Church canonizes people whose holiness is certain, but there are countless unnamed saints.',
        'Help each child see that holiness is for them too.'],
 misc=['“Saints are people who never did anything wrong.”', '“Only holy days of obligation are important.”'],
 mem='“Saints of God, come to their aid; hasten to meet them, angels of the Lord.” (from the funeral rite)',
 assess='Ask each child to share a saint and one way that saint loved God.'),
}
